{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013DXz1Lqr22SXRwJbyRZSky","type":"tool_result","content":"# ACL Rolling Review Form Rubric\n\n## Reviewer Confidence\nScale: 1-5\n- **5:** \"Positive that my evaluation is correct. I read the paper very carefully and am familiar with related work.\"\n- **4:** \"Quite sure. I tried to check the important points carefully.\"\n- **3:** \"Pretty sure, but there's a chance I missed something.\"\n- **2:** \"Willing to defend my evaluation, but it is fairly likely that I missed some details.\"\n- **1:** \"Not my area, or paper is very hard to understand.\"\n\n## Soundness\nScale: 1-5 (with .5 increments)\n- **5:** Exceptional thoroughness for its paper type\n- **4:** \"Strong: This study provides sufficient support for all of its claims.\"\n- **3:** \"Acceptable: This study provides sufficient support for its main claims.\"\n- **2:** \"Poor: Some of the main claims are not sufficiently supported.\"\n- **1:** \"Major Issues: This study is not yet sufficiently thorough to warrant publication.\"\n\n## Excitement\nScale: 1-5 (with .5 increments)\n- **5:** \"Highly Exciting: I would recommend this paper to others and/or attend its presentation.\"\n- **4:** \"Exciting: I would mention this paper to others and/or make an effort to attend.\"\n- **3:** \"Interesting: I might mention some points of this paper to others.\"\n- **2:** \"Potentially Interesting: this paper does not resonate with me.\"\n- **1:** \"Not Exciting: this paper does not resonate with me, and I don't think it would with others.\"\n\n## Overall Assessment\nScale: 1-5 (with .5 increments)\n- **5:** \"Consider for Award\" (top 2.5% papers)\n- **4.5:** Borderline Award\n- **4:** \"Conference: I think this paper could be accepted.\"\n- **3.5:** Borderline Conference\n- **3:** \"Findings: I think this paper could be accepted to the Findings.\"\n- **2.5:** Borderline Findings\n- **2:** \"Resubmit next cycle\"\n- **1.5:** \"Resubmit after next cycle\"\n- **1:** \"Do not resubmit\"\n\n## Reproducibility\nScale: 1-5\n- **5:** Results easily reproducible\n- **4:** Mostly reproducible with minor variations possible\n- **3:** \"Reproducible with some difficulty\" due to underspecified parameters\n- **2:** Results would be difficult to reproduce without author access\n- **1:** Results cannot be reproduced\n\n## Datasets & Software\nScale: 1-5\n- **5:** Enabling—should influence research directions\n- **4:** Useful—recommendable for ongoing work\n- **3:** Potentially useful\n- **2:** Documentary value for replication only\n- **1:** No usable materials released"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d5948ce3-0bd6-4774-9a29-31d903f57111","uuid":"97f746c1-7566-409b-aa78-06525ebd5a77","timestamp":"2026-05-13T09:54:47.631Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":25365,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# ACL Rolling Review Form Rubric\n\n## Reviewer Confidence\nScale: 1-5\n- **5:** \"Positive that my evaluation is correct. I read the paper very carefully and am familiar with related work.\"\n- **4:** \"Quite sure. I tried to check the important points carefully.\"\n- **3:** \"Pretty sure, but there's a chance I missed something.\"\n- **2:** \"Willing to defend my evaluation, but it is fairly likely that I missed some details.\"\n- **1:** \"Not my area, or paper is very hard to understand.\"\n\n## Soundness\nScale: 1-5 (with .5 increments)\n- **5:** Exceptional thoroughness for its paper type\n- **4:** \"Strong: This study provides sufficient support for all of its claims.\"\n- **3:** \"Acceptable: This study provides sufficient support for its main claims.\"\n- **2:** \"Poor: Some of the main claims are not sufficiently supported.\"\n- **1:** \"Major Issues: This study is not yet sufficiently thorough to warrant publication.\"\n\n## Excitement\nScale: 1-5 (with .5 increments)\n- **5:** \"Highly Exciting: I would recommend this paper to others and/or attend its presentation.\"\n- **4:** \"Exciting: I would mention this paper to others and/or make an effort to attend.\"\n- **3:** \"Interesting: I might mention some points of this paper to others.\"\n- **2:** \"Potentially Interesting: this paper does not resonate with me.\"\n- **1:** \"Not Exciting: this paper does not resonate with me, and I don't think it would with others.\"\n\n## Overall Assessment\nScale: 1-5 (with .5 increments)\n- **5:** \"Consider for Award\" (top 2.5% papers)\n- **4.5:** Borderline Award\n- **4:** \"Conference: I think this paper could be accepted.\"\n- **3.5:** Borderline Conference\n- **3:** \"Findings: I think this paper could be accepted to the Findings.\"\n- **2.5:** Borderline Findings\n- **2:** \"Resubmit next cycle\"\n- **1.5:** \"Resubmit after next cycle\"\n- **1:** \"Do not resubmit\"\n\n## Reproducibility\nScale: 1-5\n- **5:** Results easily reproducible\n- **4:** Mostly reproducible with minor variations possible\n- **3:** \"Reproducible with some difficulty\" due to underspecified parameters\n- **2:** Results would be difficult to reproduce without author access\n- **1:** Results cannot be reproduced\n\n## Datasets & Software\nScale: 1-5\n- **5:** Enabling—should influence research directions\n- **4:** Useful—recommendable for ongoing work\n- **3:** Potentially useful\n- **2:** Documentary value for replication only\n- **1:** No usable materials released","durationMs":7966,"url":"https://aclrollingreview.org/reviewform"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018miNUwaposi1jawvvURTuX","type":"tool_result","content":"# ARR Reviewer Guidelines: Scoring and Evaluation\n\n## Scoring System Overview\n\nARR reviewers provide three distinct scores starting February 2025:\n\n1. **Soundness** - Technical validity and methodological rigor\n2. **Excitement** - Personal interest and subjective appeal\n3. **Overall Recommendation** - Composite assessment for venue suitability\n\n## Soundness Score Guidance\n\nThe guidelines emphasize that \"soundness scores must be justified by the text of the review.\" Low soundness ratings without documented flaws indicate the review lacks faithful explanation of recommendations. Reviewers should examine whether they're relying on unconscious bias rather than substantive technical concerns.\n\n## Excitement Score Guidance\n\nThis orthogonal metric captures personal preference independent of technical merit. The guidelines note: \"Excitement scores reflect our personal taste, and so do not necessarily come with explicit reasons.\" A paper can be technically sound yet personally unexciting to a reviewer.\n\n## Overall Recommendation Guidance\n\nThis composite score reflects soundness, excitement, novelty, and impact combined. Critically, \"all papers recommended for Findings and main conferences are expected to be sufficiently sound and reproducible.\"\n\n## Findings vs. Main Conference Distinction\n\nThe guidelines indicate reviewers may recommend papers for main conference venues \"even if you personally are not excited about it.\" Examples include efficiency improvements or high-quality resources for underrepresented languages/domains—impactful contributions despite limited novelty or personal interest.\n\n**Note:** The document doesn't specify numerical rubrics or exact score thresholds for each level."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d5948ce3-0bd6-4774-9a29-31d903f57111","uuid":"b23dc2b5-2740-4d8d-8908-49aea2a5d95a","timestamp":"2026-05-13T09:54:48.372Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":100603,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# ARR Reviewer Guidelines: Scoring and Evaluation\n\n## Scoring System Overview\n\nARR reviewers provide three distinct scores starting February 2025:\n\n1. **Soundness** - Technical validity and methodological rigor\n2. **Excitement** - Personal interest and subjective appeal\n3. **Overall Recommendation** - Composite assessment for venue suitability\n\n## Soundness Score Guidance\n\nThe guidelines emphasize that \"soundness scores must be justified by the text of the review.\" Low soundness ratings without documented flaws indicate the review lacks faithful explanation of recommendations. Reviewers should examine whether they're relying on unconscious bias rather than substantive technical concerns.\n\n## Excitement Score Guidance\n\nThis orthogonal metric captures personal preference independent of technical merit. The guidelines note: \"Excitement scores reflect our personal taste, and so do not necessarily come with explicit reasons.\" A paper can be technically sound yet personally unexciting to a reviewer.\n\n## Overall Recommendation Guidance\n\nThis composite score reflects soundness, excitement, novelty, and impact combined. Critically, \"all papers recommended for Findings and main conferences are expected to be sufficiently sound and reproducible.\"\n\n## Findings vs. Main Conference Distinction\n\nThe guidelines indicate reviewers may recommend papers for main conference venues \"even if you personally are not excited about it.\" Examples include efficiency improvements or high-quality resources for underrepresented languages/domains—impactful contributions despite limited novelty or personal interest.\n\n**Note:** The document doesn't specify numerical rubrics or exact score thresholds for each level.","durationMs":7384,"url":"https://aclrollingreview.org/reviewerguidelines"}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01A41hBQ2pF8CCrubDLeErhV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have the full ARR/EMNLP review form rubric. Let me summarize the key scoring dimensions:\n\n- **Soundness**: 1-5 (0.5 increments)\n- **Excitement**: 1-5 (0.5 increments)  \n- **Overall Assessment**: 1-5 (0.5 increments), where 3 = Findings, 3.5 = Borderline Conference, 4 = Conference\n\n\n- **Reproducibility**: 1-5\n- **Confidence**: 1-5\n\nSince the target is EMNLP 2026 Findings, I need to set up a structured review process with two specialized reviewers—one focusing on methods and experiments, the other on novelty and related work—then run them through a four-step roundtable discussion to reach consensus.\n\nI'm now preparing detailed prompts for both reviewers so they have the full paper content, scoring rubric, and clear guidance on their respective focus areas before launching them in parallel.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1473,"cache_read_input_tokens":60070,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1473},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d5948ce3-0bd6-4774-9a29-31d903f57111","uuid":"6e402b01-f4d4-4acb-822a-6c653a016e2e"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01A41hBQ2pF8CCrubDLeErhV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have the paper, the experiment context, and the ARR/EMNLP scoring rubric. Now dispatching two independent reviewers in parallel."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1473,"cache_read_input_tokens":60070,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1473},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d5948ce3-0bd6-4774-9a29-31d903f57111","uuid":"ede7d3ca-ce56-43a2-bbfc-56789af75f90"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01A41hBQ2pF8CCrubDLeErhV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01EyvyTJLeHCS14HM3aHx2sg","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a blind peer review. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**. \n\n## Venue & Scoring Rubric (EMNLP 2026 via ARR)\n\nUse these scales (0.5 increments):\n- **Soundness** (1-5): 5=Exceptional, 4=Strong (sufficient support for all claims), 3=Acceptable (sufficient for main claims), 2=Poor (main claims not sufficiently supported), 1=Major issues\n- **Excitement** (1-5): 5=Highly exciting, 4=Exciting, 3=Interesting, 2=Potentially interesting, 1=Not exciting\n- **Overall** (1-5): 5=Award, 4=Conference, 3.5=Borderline Conference, 3=Findings, 2.5=Borderline Findings, 2=Resubmit next cycle, 1.5=Resubmit after next cycle\n- **Reproducibility** (1-5): 5=Easily reproducible, 4=Mostly reproducible, 3=Some difficulty, 2=Difficult, 1=Cannot reproduce\n- **Confidence** (1-5): 5=Positive my evaluation is correct, 4=Quite sure, 3=Pretty sure, 2=Willing to defend but may have missed details, 1=Not my area\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"When Does Circuit Consensus Fail? A Systematic Analysis of Cross-Method Agreement in Mechanistic Interpretability\"\n\nThe paper formalizes a \"Circuit Selection Score\" (CSS) — the fraction of K=3 circuit discovery methods (EAP-IG, EP, ACDC) selecting each edge — and tests whether high-consensus edges (CSS=1.0) outperform individual methods. Evaluated across 4 tasks (IOI, Arithmetic, SVA, Factual Recall) × 2 models (GPT-2 Small, Qwen-0.5B) = 6 configurations. Central finding: consensus succeeds in only 1/6 cases (IOI on GPT-2, ΔCPR=+0.085, p=0.002).\n\nThree failure modes identified:\n1. Task-induced method correlation (high Jaccard > 0.5 → methods converge on same obvious bottleneck edges)\n2. Component-level scoring degeneration on Qwen-0.5B (IFR → layer-level due to GQA; ACDC → node-level due to 179K search space)\n3. Jaccard diagnostic: max pairwise Jaccard > 0.5 predicts failure with 5/6 accuracy (Spearman ρ=−0.90, p=0.037 excl. outlier)\n\nKey experimental results:\n- **Table 1**: 6 configurations, only IOI GPT-2 PASS. IOI: max_J=0.307, CSS=1.0 has 7 edges, CPR=0.109 vs best single 0.029 (ACDC), Δ=+0.085. Factual Recall: max_J=0.620, CSS=1.0 CPR=0.905 < EAP-IG 0.975, Δ=−0.070. SVA: max_J=0.653, Δ=+0.013ns. Arithmetic: max_J=0.695, Δ=−0.063. Qwen IFR: max_J=0.540, 0 CSS=1.0 edges. Qwen ACDC: max_J=0.043, 0 CSS=1.0 edges.\n- **Table 2**: Factual Recall detailed — EAP-IG 50 edges CPR=0.975, CSS=1.0 10 edges CPR=0.905.\n- **Table 3**: Factual Recall Jaccard matrix: EAP-IG×EP=0.62, ACDC×EAP-IG=0.38, ACDC×EP=0.33.\n- **Table 4**: Size-controlled IOI — CSS=1.0 (7 edges, CPR=0.109) vs Top-7 EAP-IG (0.048), Top-7 ACDC (0.042), Top-7 EP (0.035), Random-7 (0.003±0.012). 76.3% advantage retained.\n- **Table 5**: Bootstrap CIs (B=10K, BCa) — IOI Δ=+0.085 CI [0.035,0.138] p=0.002; Arithmetic Δ=−0.063 CI [−0.112,−0.018] p=0.008; Factual Recall Δ=−0.068 CI [−0.118,−0.022] p=0.005; SVA Δ=+0.013 CI [−0.025,0.052] p=0.482.\n- **Table 6**: Qwen scoring granularity — IFR=Layer (J=0.0 with gradient methods), ACDC=Node (J=0.022/0.021).\n- **Table 7**: Scale-dependent behavior GPT-2 vs Qwen.\n- **Table 8**: Jaccard diagnostic — 5/6 correct. Only miss: Qwen ACDC (low J but FAIL due to Claim 3 not Claim 4).\n- **Table 9**: IOI zero vs mean ablation — Zero Δ=+0.080, Mean Δ=+0.065, 81.3% retained.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\n\nThe imagined experiment plan includes 5 experiments:\n1. GPT-2 Factual Recall K=3 CSS (v2) — covered in paper\n2. IOI Size-Controlled Baseline — covered in paper (Table 4)\n3. Bootstrap Confidence Intervals — covered in paper (Table 5)\n4. Ablation Method Comparison (Zero vs Mean) on IOI — covered in paper (Table 9)\n5. IFR vs ACDC vs Gradient Method Divergence Analysis — covered in paper (Table 6, §5.2)\n6. Jaccard-CSS Diagnostic Scatter Plot + Correlation — covered in paper (Table 8, §5.3)\n\nAll 5 planned experiments appear to be reflected in the paper.\n\n## Your Review Focus (Methods + Experiments)\n\nEvaluate:\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Is CSS well-defined? Is the binarization (top-n) approach justified? Are evaluation metrics (CPR, CMD) appropriate?\n2. **Experimental design**: Is the setup sufficient to support claims? Are controls adequate? Any missing baselines?\n3. **Statistical rigor**: Are bootstrap CIs appropriate? Sample sizes adequate? Multiple testing issues?\n4. **Reproducibility**: Is there enough detail to reproduce? Are hyperparameters specified?\n5. **Edge count confound**: The paper acknowledges CSS=1.0 has far fewer edges than individual methods (7 vs 50-100). Is the size-controlled analysis sufficient?\n6. **Evaluation metrics**: CPR is the primary metric. Is CMD sufficiently discussed? Are there other metrics that should be considered?\n7. **Binarization sensitivity**: Does the choice of top-n affect conclusions? Is this explored?\n\n## Output Format\n\nProvide your review in this structure:\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods & Experiments\n\n### Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\n### Strengths\n- S1: ...\n- S2: ...\n- S3: ...\n\n### Weaknesses\n- W1: ...\n- W2: ...\n- W3: ...\n\n### Questions for Authors\n- Q1: ...\n- Q2: ...\n\n### Minor Issues\n- ...\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Overall: X/5\n- Reproducibility: X/5\n- Confidence: X/5\n```\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1473,"cache_read_input_tokens":60070,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1473},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d5948ce3-0bd6-4774-9a29-31d903f57111","uuid":"6b77d5a3-f662-419f-83df-9b0fd7090b0a"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a10e5aef12332efbc","tool_use_id":"toolu_01EyvyTJLeHCS14HM3aHx2sg","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a blind peer review. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**. \n\n## Venue & Scoring Rubric (EMNLP 2026 via ARR)\n\nUse these scales (0.5 increments):\n- **Soundness** (1-5): 5=Exceptional, 4=Strong (sufficient support for all claims), 3=Acceptable (sufficient for main claims), 2=Poor (main claims not sufficiently supported), 1=Major issues\n- **Excitement** (1-5): 5=Highly exciting, 4=Exciting, 3=Interesting, 2=Potentially interesting, 1=Not exciting\n- **Overall** (1-5): 5=Award, 4=Conference, 3.5=Borderline Conference, 3=Findings, 2.5=Borderline Findings, 2=Resubmit next cycle, 1.5=Resubmit after next cycle\n- **Reproducibility** (1-5): 5=Easily reproducible, 4=Mostly reproducible, 3=Some difficulty, 2=Difficult, 1=Cannot reproduce\n- **Confidence** (1-5): 5=Positive my evaluation is correct, 4=Quite sure, 3=Pretty sure, 2=Willing to defend but may have missed details, 1=Not my area\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"When Does Circuit Consensus Fail? A Systematic Analysis of Cross-Method Agreement in Mechanistic Interpretability\"\n\nThe paper formalizes a \"Circuit Selection Score\" (CSS) — the fraction of K=3 circuit discovery methods (EAP-IG, EP, ACDC) selecting each edge — and tests whether high-consensus edges (CSS=1.0) outperform individual methods. Evaluated across 4 tasks (IOI, Arithmetic, SVA, Factual Recall) × 2 models (GPT-2 Small, Qwen-0.5B) = 6 configurations. Central finding: consensus succeeds in only 1/6 cases (IOI on GPT-2, ΔCPR=+0.085, p=0.002).\n\nThree failure modes identified:\n1. Task-induced method correlation (high Jaccard > 0.5 → methods converge on same obvious bottleneck edges)\n2. Component-level scoring degeneration on Qwen-0.5B (IFR → layer-level due to GQA; ACDC → node-level due to 179K search space)\n3. Jaccard diagnostic: max pairwise Jaccard > 0.5 predicts failure with 5/6 accuracy (Spearman ρ=−0.90, p=0.037 excl. outlier)\n\nKey experimental results:\n- **Table 1**: 6 configurations, only IOI GPT-2 PASS. IOI: max_J=0.307, CSS=1.0 has 7 edges, CPR=0.109 vs best single 0.029 (ACDC), Δ=+0.085. Factual Recall: max_J=0.620, CSS=1.0 CPR=0.905 < EAP-IG 0.975, Δ=−0.070. SVA: max_J=0.653, Δ=+0.013ns. Arithmetic: max_J=0.695, Δ=−0.063. Qwen IFR: max_J=0.540, 0 CSS=1.0 edges. Qwen ACDC: max_J=0.043, 0 CSS=1.0 edges.\n- **Table 2**: Factual Recall detailed — EAP-IG 50 edges CPR=0.975, CSS=1.0 10 edges CPR=0.905.\n- **Table 3**: Factual Recall Jaccard matrix: EAP-IG×EP=0.62, ACDC×EAP-IG=0.38, ACDC×EP=0.33.\n- **Table 4**: Size-controlled IOI — CSS=1.0 (7 edges, CPR=0.109) vs Top-7 EAP-IG (0.048), Top-7 ACDC (0.042), Top-7 EP (0.035), Random-7 (0.003±0.012). 76.3% advantage retained.\n- **Table 5**: Bootstrap CIs (B=10K, BCa) — IOI Δ=+0.085 CI [0.035,0.138] p=0.002; Arithmetic Δ=−0.063 CI [−0.112,−0.018] p=0.008; Factual Recall Δ=−0.068 CI [−0.118,−0.022] p=0.005; SVA Δ=+0.013 CI [−0.025,0.052] p=0.482.\n- **Table 6**: Qwen scoring granularity — IFR=Layer (J=0.0 with gradient methods), ACDC=Node (J=0.022/0.021).\n- **Table 7**: Scale-dependent behavior GPT-2 vs Qwen.\n- **Table 8**: Jaccard diagnostic — 5/6 correct. Only miss: Qwen ACDC (low J but FAIL due to Claim 3 not Claim 4).\n- **Table 9**: IOI zero vs mean ablation — Zero Δ=+0.080, Mean Δ=+0.065, 81.3% retained.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\n\nThe imagined experiment plan includes 5 experiments:\n1. GPT-2 Factual Recall K=3 CSS (v2) — covered in paper\n2. IOI Size-Controlled Baseline — covered in paper (Table 4)\n3. Bootstrap Confidence Intervals — covered in paper (Table 5)\n4. Ablation Method Comparison (Zero vs Mean) on IOI — covered in paper (Table 9)\n5. IFR vs ACDC vs Gradient Method Divergence Analysis — covered in paper (Table 6, §5.2)\n6. Jaccard-CSS Diagnostic Scatter Plot + Correlation — covered in paper (Table 8, §5.3)\n\nAll 5 planned experiments appear to be reflected in the paper.\n\n## Your Review Focus (Methods + Experiments)\n\nEvaluate:\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Is CSS well-defined? Is the binarization (top-n) approach justified? Are evaluation metrics (CPR, CMD) appropriate?\n2. **Experimental design**: Is the setup sufficient to support claims? Are controls adequate? Any missing baselines?\n3. **Statistical rigor**: Are bootstrap CIs appropriate? Sample sizes adequate? Multiple testing issues?\n4. **Reproducibility**: Is there enough detail to reproduce? Are hyperparameters specified?\n5. **Edge count confound**: The paper acknowledges CSS=1.0 has far fewer edges than individual methods (7 vs 50-100). Is the size-controlled analysis sufficient?\n6. **Evaluation metrics**: CPR is the primary metric. Is CMD sufficiently discussed? Are there other metrics that should be considered?\n7. **Binarization sensitivity**: Does the choice of top-n affect conclusions? Is this explored?\n\n## Output Format\n\nProvide your review in this structure:\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods & Experiments\n\n### Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\n### Strengths\n- S1: ...\n- S2: ...\n- S3: ...\n\n### Weaknesses\n- W1: ...\n- W2: ...\n- W3: ...\n\n### Questions for Authors\n- Q1: ...\n- Q2: ...\n\n### Minor Issues\n- ...\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Overall: X/5\n- Reproducibility: X/5\n- Confidence: X/5\n```\n","uuid":"4719d341-1181-4752-bec7-fd99060881f6","session_id":"d5948ce3-0bd6-4774-9a29-31d903f57111"}
